.TH FORTUNE 6.SH NAMEfortune, strfile, unstr \- sayings at random, and their index.SH SYNOPSIS\fBfortune\fR[\-wsloa] [file].br\fBstrfile\fR[\-cC] [\-sv] input [datafile].br\fBunstr\fRdatafile[.dat] [outfile].SH DESCRIPTION\fBfortune\fRprints a saying chosen at random:.TP 12\fB\-s\fRa short one..TP 12\fB\-l\fRa long one..TP 12\fB\-o\fRfrom the offensive file..TP 12\fB\-a\fRfrom either..TP 12\fB\-w\fRwait long enough to read it before returning..PPA file named reads that collection instead of the usual one..PP\fBstrfile\fRbuilds the index\fBfortune\fRreads.  Its input is sayings separated by lines holding only\fB%%\fR;\fRit writes\fIinput\fB.dat\fRand reports how many strings there were and the longest and shortest.\fB\-c\fRnames another delimiting character,\fB\-s\fRkeeps it quiet..PP\fBunstr\fRis the reverse: it writes the sayings back out of a\fB.dat\fRfile as text..SH FILES.TP 12\fB/dd/GAMES/FORTUNE/fortunes.dat\fRthe sayings..SH NOTES\fBstrfile\fRneeds Microware's\fBcio\fR.\fR.SH SEE ALSOcookie